    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": " \n                          VETO MESSAGE - No. 94\n \nTO THE SENATE:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nSenate Bill Number 7001, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to amend the state finance law, in relation to contracting\n      between state agencies and not-for-profit  organizations;  and  to\n      repeal  subdivision 7 of section 179-v of such law relating there-\n      to\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This bill would overhaul the process by which not-for-profit organiza-\ntions contract with New York State. Among other things, the bill  would:\nrequire  advance  payments  on all contracts to not-for-profit entities;\nmandate quarterly advances and payment of interest on delayed contracts;\nset a minimum 15% indirect rate on all contracts; require state agencies\nto reimburse not-for-profits for interest accrued on  lines  of  credit;\nand  set timelines for contract modifications. The stated purpose of the\nbill is to allow for not-for-profit organizations to receive funds  more\nexpediently and to cover costs incurred due to contracting delays.\n \n  This  bill  raises  several  concerns.  Most  notably,  the mandate to\nprovide advance payments could run afoul of the  state  Constitution  by\nrequiring  expenditures  that  precede the appropriations that authorize\nthem. This requirement could also result in payments  to  providers  who\nhave  not  been  fully  vetted and determined to be responsible vendors.\nAdditionally, several provisions run afoul of federal  requirements  for\nfederally-funded grants. Finally, the bill would likely impose a signif-\nicant  cost  to  the  state  by  making interest payments mandatory when\ncontracts are late, even in cases where the delay  is  not  due  to  the\nfault of the state agency.\n\n  I  recognize the vital work not-for-profit organizations do to provide\nservices to New Yorkers and remain committed to finding ways to expedite\nthe processing of contracts. I have directed my Office of Customer Expe-\nrience to identify administrative burdens that can be reduced  in  order\nto  more  swiftly  fund  not-for-profit organizations that provide vital\nservices to the state and its residents. However, this bill raises seri-\nous legal and fiscal concerns and increases the risk of fraud and  abuse\nof state funds.\n \n  Therefore, I am constrained to veto this bill.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                       (signed) KATHY HOCHUL\n \n                               __________\n",
